  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Chef loves palindromes!\n\nA palindrome is a string that reads the same forward and backward. For example, \"racecar\"\\texttt{\"racecar\"}\"racecar\" and \"aibohphobia\"\\texttt{\"aibohphobia\"}\"aibohphobia\" are examples of palindromes, while \"fly\"\\texttt{\"fly\"}\"fly\" and \"palindrome\"\\texttt{\"palindrome\"}\"palindrome\" are not.\n\nChef has with him a string SSS of length NNN, containing only lowercase English letters.\n\nHe wants to turn SSS into a palindrome. To do that, he can perform the following operation:\n\n\nChoose a substring of SSS that's not a palindrome and doesn't equal the full string, and delete this substring from SSS. The remaining characters don't change their order.\nIs it possible for Chef to turn SSS into a palindrome by using several (possibly, zero) of these operations?\n\nIf it is, also find the minimum number of operations necessary.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of two lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains a single integer NNN — the length of the string SSS.\nThe second line contains a string SSS of length NNN, containing only lowercase English letters.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, print the answer on a new line:\n\n\n\nIf it's not possible to turn SSS into a palindrome no matter what, print the integer −1-1−1.\nOtherwise, print the minimum number of operations Chef needs.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "1≤N≤2⋅1051 \\leq N \\leq 2\\cdot 10^51≤N≤2⋅105",
      "SiS_iSi​ is one of the characters `'a', 'b', 'c', ..., 'z'`.",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases won't exceed 2⋅1052\\cdot 10^52⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n7\nracecar\n2\nmy\n5\nmodem",
        "output": "0\n-1\n1",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: The string is already a palindrome, no operations are necessary.\nTest case 222: The string is not a palindrome, but Chef also has no valid moves - there's no substring that's both not a palindrome and also not equal to the full string.\nSo, it's impossible to change the string, and the answer is −1-1−1.\nTest case 333: Chef can delete the substring od\\texttt{od}od comprising indices 222 and 333.\nThis turns the string into \"mem\"\\texttt{\"mem\"}\"mem\", which is a palindrome."
      }
    ]
  },
